by HypeMC at 2026-10-15T10:45:01Z

@nicolas-grekas [Done](https://github.com/symfony/symfony/compare/ca446c14ccdc8177c0c4227354c4680f2e6bed37..93af44e647232b72eb2d3e3a87549774f495aa66). The cooldown now respects `RecoverableExceptionInterface::getRetryDelay()`, using the same logic as the retry listener via the shared `RetryDecider::getDelayFromException()`.

The first four consecutive 429s still don't trigger a cooldown. The fifth does, and the cooldown lasts at least as long as the requested retry delay.

This can exceed `max_delay`, which defaults to 60s. The trade-off is that a message-specific retry delay now pauses the entire transport, potentially for a long time. Such messages are better handled by a separate transport.

If `max_delay` should remain a hard cap, that's easy to adjust, but your 2-minute example would then require increasing it.

